4\.1\.2\.a Name,Role, Value

__Overview__

- General \- The name, role and value of user interface components \(controls\) must be programmatically determinable by assistive technologies\.
- HTML \- The name, role and value of user interface components \(controls\) must be programmatically determinable by assistive technologies\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

See Assistive Technology testing procedure\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

In general, you will find name, role, state, or value issues under this checkpoint related to HTML elements or custom components that have an HTML equivalent such as links, buttons, checkboxes, radio buttons, and selects\. For name, role, state, or value issues related to more complex widgets, especially ones that relate to existing ARIA authoring patterns such as tabpanels, dialogs, listboxes, sliders, carousels, etc, see the issues under checkpoint 4\.1\.2\.b Custom Controls\.

Use checkpoint 4\.1\.2\.c Compatibility to log issues for complex interactions that don't have HTML or ARIA widget equivalents and are so broken that breaking them down into individual issues is not effective or productive\.

__Screen reader testing is required for this checkpoint\.__

Using a screen reader:

1. Sequentially go through all the content in the page\.
2. Access all user interface components one by one, relying only on the screen reader\.
3. Make sure the screen reader correctly conveys the following information about each component: 
	- __Its role:__ e\.g\., button, link, checkbox, radio button, text field, tab, slider, switch, combobox, etc\.
	- __Its name:__ e\.g\. a label for a form control or button, the name of the tab, the label of a checkbox, etc\.
	- __If applicable, its value or state:__ e\.g\., selected, pressed, disabled, expanded/collapsed, on/off, text field's value, slider's value, etc\.

__NOTE: __ARIA authoring patterns \(ARIA attributes and keyboard patterns\) are not normative \(i\.e\. are not required for WCAG conformance\)\. Elements and widgets can meet 4\.1\.2 without following an ARIA pattern exactly AS LONG AS the element or widget provides the needed name, role, values, and states for a screen reader user\. These states and properties may be provided via HTML or ARIA\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- An AREA element has no text alternative\. \(alt\-text\-area\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- Informative text \(static text\) is implemented with an interactive role\. \(android\-active\-role\-inappropriate, "issue", moderate\)
- A control looks like an AlertDialog but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-alert, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a BadgeDrawable but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-badge, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a BottomNavigationView but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-bottom\-nav, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a CheckBox but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-checkbox, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a CompoundButton but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-compound\-button, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like an EditText but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-edit\-text, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like an ExpandableListView but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-expandable\-list, "issue", serious\)
- A control opens Chrome but is missing the link role\. \(android\-control\-link, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a Menu but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-menu, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a PopupMenu but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-popup\-menu, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a ProgressBar but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-progress\-bar, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a RadioButton but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-radio\-button, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a RatingBar but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-rating\-bar, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a SeekBar but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-seek\-bar, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a SnackBar but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-snack\-bar, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a Spinner but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-spinner, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a Switch but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-switch, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a TabHost but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-tab\-host, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a Toast but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-toast, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a ToggleButton but doesn't behave like one\. \(android\-control\-toggle\-button, "issue", serious\)
- Nesting an anchor or button inside another anchor or button fails validation and may cause troubles for keyboard and screen reader users\. \(button\-link\-nested, "issue", moderate\)
- The button does not have a role\. \(button\-missing\-role, "issue", serious\)
- The button is missing an accessible name/label\. As a result its functionality or purpose is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(button\-name\-inaccessible, "issue", critical\)
- The button is missing both a role and an accessible name/label\. \(button\-role\-name\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The disabled state of an element is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(button\-state\-disabled, "issue", serious\)
- The pressed state of a button is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(button\-state\-pressed, "issue", serious\)
- The selected state of a button is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(button\-state\-selected, "issue", serious\)
- The custom checkbox element is missing a role and/or checked state\. \(checkbox\-missing\-role\-state, "issue", critical\)
- The control has an incorrect role\. \(control\-incorrect\-role, "issue", serious\)
- The control has an incorrect state\. \(control\-incorrect\-state, "issue", serious\)
- The control is missing a role\. \(control\-missing\-role, "issue", critical\)
- The control is missing a necessary state\. \(control\-missing\-state, "issue", serious\)
- The control is missing an accessible name/label\. \(control\-name\-inaccessible, "issue", critical\)
- The control is missing both a role and an accessible name/label\. \(control\-role\-name\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The disabled state of a control is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(control\-state\-disabled, "issue", serious\)
- The expand/collapse state of a toggle\-type element is missing or is used incorrectly\. \(expand\-collapse\-state, "issue", serious\)
- Form control is not programmatically associated with its visible label\. \(form\-field\-missing\-label, "issue", critical\)
- The group of form elements grouped using ARIA role="group" or "radiogroup" has no group\-level label \(similar to an HTML legend\)\. \(form\-group\-not\-labeled, "issue", serious\)
- The form field does not have an explicit or implicit LABEL relationship, nor a title, aria\-labelledby, aria\-label, or placeholder attribute\. \(form\-label\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- Informative text \(static text\) is implemented with an interactive role\. \(ios\-active\-role\-inappropriate, "issue", moderate\)
- Actionable item lacks a role or semantic markup to indicate it is actionable \(e\.g\. listview or tableview cell\)\. \(ios\-control\-actionable, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UIAlertController but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-alert, "issue", serious\)
- A custom control is not accessible\. \(ios\-control\-custom, "issue", serious\)
- The custom checkbox control is not accessible\. \(ios\-control\-custom\-checkbox, "issue", serious\)
- The custom radio button control is not accessible\. \(ios\-control\-custom\-radio\-button, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UIDatePicker but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-date\-picker, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like an editable UITextView but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-editable\-textview, "issue", serious\)
- A control opens Safari but is missing the link trait\. \(ios\-control\-link, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UIPageControl but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-page\-control, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UIPickerView but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-picker, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UIPopoverPresentationController but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-popover, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UIProgressView but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-progress, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UISearchBar but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-search, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UISegmentedControl but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-segmented\-control, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UISlider but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-slider, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UIStepper but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-stepper, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UISwitch but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-switch, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UITabBar but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-tab\-bar, "issue", serious\)
- A control looks like a UITextField but doesn't behave like one\. \(ios\-control\-text\-field, "issue", serious\)
- The link does not have a role\. \(link\-missing\-role, "issue", serious\)
- The link is missing accessible link text\. As a result its destination or purpose is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(link\-missing\-text, "issue", critical\)
- The custom radio button element is missing a role and/or selected state\. \(radio\-missing\-role\-state, "issue", critical\)
- The custom select element is missing required roles and/or states or properties\. \(select\-missing\-role\-state, "issue", critical\)
- The expand/collapse state of a toggle\-type element is missing or is used incorrectly\. \(state\-aria\-expanded\-missing\-incorrect, "issue", serious\)
- The selected state of an element is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(state\-selected\-missing\-incorrect, "issue", serious\)
- The sorted state \(ascending/descending\) of table columns is missing or incorrect\. \(state\-table\-sort, "issue", serious\)
- The iframe is missing a title\. \(title\-iframe\-missing, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- ARIA for tabs, modals and other dynamic widgets \- ARIA should be used to indicate name, role and value for tabs, modals or other dynamic widgets \(aria\-not\-used, "best\-practice", moderate, May 28, 2013\)
- Provide labels for ARIA regions \- Provide aria\-labels to describe sections with role="region"\. \(aria\-region\-not\-labeled, "best\-practice", moderate, February 28, 2019\)
- Use button to activate scripted functionality \- Use a button to allow users to activate scripted functionality \(form submission, play/pause, etc\.\)\. \(button\-acts\-like\-link, "best\-practice", moderate, June 26, 2017\)
- Button should look and act like a button \- If an element looks like a button, it should act like a button \(activate scripted functionality\) and be a button \(or have a role of button\)\. \(button\-looks\-like\-link, "best\-practice", moderate, June 26, 2017\)
- Avoid duplicate LABEL and TITLE \- Explicit labels should be provided using the LABEL element\. Avoid using a TITLE attribute that duplicates the LABEL\. \(form\-title\-duplicates\-label, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Use an explicit form label \- Associate the label with the corresponding form element using the explicit for/id method\. \(label\-implicit, "best\-practice", minor, April 24, 2017\)
- Avoid orphaned form labels \- There should not be any orphaned form labels\. Each form LABEL should be associated with its corresponding form element\. \(label\-orphaned, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Avoid duplicate label on a button \- Avoid duplicate label on a button\. The accessible name of an input button can natively be set by the input type or by the value attribute\. Avoid using the label element or title attribute on a button that already has an accessible name\. \(label\-redundant\-button, "best\-practice", minor, May 9, 2018\)
- Use an implicit/explicit label, or aria\-label or aria\-labelledby attribute to programmatically label fields \- Label input fields using a method that programmatically defines their accessible name \(implicit/explicit label, or aria\-label or aria\-labelledby attribute\), as opposed to accessible description \(title or aria\-describedby attribute\)\. \(label\-title\-only, "best\-practice", minor, May 16, 2017\)
- Use link to change focus or go to new URL \- Use a link to allow users to activate the user\-agent to change focus or go to a new URL\. \(link\-acts\-like\-button, "best\-practice", moderate, June 26, 2017\)
- Link should look and act like a link \- If an element looks like a link, it should act like a link \(change browser focus or URL\) and be a link \(or have a role of link\)\. \(link\-looks\-like\-button, "best\-practice", moderate, June 26, 2017\)
- Provide an accessible name for content section \- Some types of content sections can benefit from having an accessible name\. For example, a tabpanel is a section of content that appears after a user activates the associated tab element\. The tab is the control and must have an accessible name\. The tabpanel is the child \(content section\) of the tab\. Adding aria\-labelledby to the tabpanel is a best practice\. \(name\-section, "best\-practice", moderate, October 9, 2017\)

4\.1\.2\.b Custom Controls

__Overview__

- General \- Complex components must meet code specifications appropriate for the platform and facilitate accessibility support with assistive technologies\.
- HTML \- Complex components must meet code specifications appropriate for the platform and facilitate accessibility support with assistive technologies\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

See Assistive Technology testing procedure\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

In general, you will find name, role, state, or value issues under this checkpoint related to more complex widgets, especially ones that relate to existing ARIA authoring patterns such as tabpanels, dialogs, listboxes, sliders, carousels, etc\. For name, role, state, or value issues related to HTML elements and custom components that have an HTML equivalent such as links, buttons, checkboxes, radio buttons, and selects, see the issues under checkpoint 4\.1\.2\.a Name, Role, Value\.

Use checkpoint 4\.1\.2\.c Compatibility to log issues for complex interactions that don't have HTML or ARIA widget equivalents and are so broken that breaking them down into individual issues is not effective or productive\.

__Screen reader testing is required for this checkpoint\.__

Using a screen reader:

1. Sequentially go through all the content in the page\.
2. Access all user interface components one by one, relying only on the screen reader\.
3. Make sure the screen reader correctly conveys the following information about each component: 
	- __Its role:__ e\.g\., button, link, checkbox, radio button, text field, tab, slider, switch, combobox, etc\.
	- __Its name:__ e\.g\. a label for a form control or button, the name of the tab, the label of a checkbox, etc\.
	- __If applicable, its value or state:__ e\.g\., selected, pressed, disabled, expanded/collapsed, on/off, text field's value, slider's value, etc\.

__NOTE: __ARIA authoring patterns \(ARIA attributes and keyboard patterns\) are not normative \(i\.e\. are not required for WCAG conformance\)\. Elements and widgets can meet 4\.1\.2 without following an ARIA pattern exactly AS LONG AS the element or widget provides the needed name, role, values, and states for a screen reader user\. These states and properties may be provided via HTML or ARIA\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The element has an invalid ARIA attribute \(a misspelled attribute or one that does not exist\)\. \(aria\-attribute\-invalid, "issue", critical\)
- The element is missing an ARIA attribute that is required for its role\. \(aria\-attribute\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The element's ARIA attribute has an invalid value \(a misspelled value or one that is not valid for the attribute\)\. \(aria\-attribute\-value\-invalid, "issue", critical\)
- The element's accessible name is missing or is not appropriate for the element's purpose\. \(aria\-name\-missing\-incorrect, "issue", critical\)
- The element's ARIA role requires that other elements with specific roles be contained within it\. At least one child role is missing\. \(aria\-required\-child\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The element's ARIA role requires that it be contained inside a parent element with a specific role\. The parent role is missing\. \(aria\-required\-parent\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The element's role attribute has an invalid value \(a misspelled value or one that does not exist\)\. \(aria\-role\-invalid, "issue", critical\)
- The element's role is missing or is not appropriate for the element's function\. \(aria\-role\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The element has missing or incorrect states or properties that are necessary for screen reader users to interact with or understand the content conveyed by the element\. \(aria\-state\-property\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like an accordion \(show/hide toggles\) but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-accordion, "issue", serious\)
- The breadcrumb region is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-breadcrumb, "issue", moderate\)
- The state of the currently displayed carousel slide is indicated visually, but this is not conveyed to screen reader users\. \(custom\-carousel\-current, "issue", moderate\)
- The total number of slides in the carousel is indicated visually, but this is not conveyed to screen readers users\. \(custom\-carousel\-number, "issue", moderate\)
- The element appears and functions like a combobox but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-combobox, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a date picker but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-date\-picker, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a modal dialog but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-dialog, "issue", moderate\)
- The element appears and functions like a grid widget but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-grid, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a listbox but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-listbox, "issue", critical\)
- Site navigation or application\-style menu is missing required roles and/or attributes\. \(custom\-navigation, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a progressbar but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-progressbar, "issue", critical\)
- role="application" is used inappropriately\. \(custom\-role\-application, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a slider but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-slider, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a switch but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-switch, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a tab/tabpanel interface but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-tabs, "issue", moderate\)
- The element appears and functions like a toolbar but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-toolbar, "issue", critical\)
- Tooltip content is not accessible to screen readers\. \(custom\-tooltip, "issue", critical\)
- The element appears and functions like a treeview but is missing required ARIA role\(s\) and/or attribute\(s\)\. \(custom\-treeview, "issue", critical\)
- The element functions as if it has two roles\. Elements are allowed to have only one functioning role\. \(dual\-role, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Custom ARIA button should activate with enter and spacebar \- An element that visually appears like a button and has an ARIA role of button should function the same as a native button\. The custom button should activate with enter and spacebar\. \(aria\-custom\-button\-spacebar, "best\-practice", serious, August 26, 2016\)
- ARIA for tabs, modals and other dynamic widgets \- ARIA should be used to indicate name, role and value for tabs, modals or other dynamic widgets \(aria\-not\-used, "best\-practice", moderate, May 28, 2013\)

4\.1\.2\.c Compatibility

__Overview__

- General \- Features and components must be compatible with assistive technologies\.
- HTML \- Features and components must be compatible with assistive technologies\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Same procedure as Assistive Technology\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Use checkpoint 4\.1\.2\.c Compatibility for complex interactions that don't have HTML or ARIA widget equivalents and are so broken that breaking them down into individual issues is not effective or productive\.

__Screen reader testing is required for this checkpoint\.__

Using a screen reader:

1. Sequentially go through all the content in the page\.
2. Access all user interface components one by one, relying only on the screen reader\.
3. Make sure the screen reader correctly conveys the following information about each component: 
	- __Its role:__ e\.g\., button, link, checkbox, radio button, text field, tab, slider, switch, combobox, etc\.
	- __Its name:__ e\.g\. a label for a form control or button, the name of the tab, the label of a checkbox, etc\.
	- __If applicable, its value or state:__ e\.g\., selected, pressed, disabled, expanded/collapsed, on/off, text field's value, slider's value, etc\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Custom user interface component is not compatible with AT \(see issue Details for more info\)\. \(custom\-not\-compatible, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

1\.3\.2\.a Reading Order 

__Overview__

- General \- When the meaning of content depends on the order in which it is read, the reading order must be programmatically determinable by assistive technology\.
- HTML \- When the meaning of content depends on the order in which it is read, the reading order must be programmatically determinable by assistive technology\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__If dynamic content is present, screen reader testing is required for this checkpoint, and highly recommended in all scenarios\.__

1. Using NVDA start at the top of the page, or portion of the page, being tested and browse down the content using the down arrow key\. 
	- Ensure that the reading order of the static content is consistent with the intended meaning of the content\.
	- Ensure that content that is meant to be hidden from all users is hidden from the screen reader\.
2. Start back at the top of the content being tested\. Browse down to controls that add or delete content from the page\. As you encounter these controls, activate the control\. 
	- Ensure the reading order of the new content is logical within the workflow of the page interactions\.
	- Specifically for modals, activate the modal and ensure that screen reader browsing is maintained within the modal while it is open\. 
		- __Note:__ For an element to be considered a modal dialog and therefore keep focus while the element is open, it must be truly "modal"\. That means that while the modal dialog is open, users cannot interact with content outside the element\. Content outside an active dialog is typically visually obscured or dimmed\. Popups, tooltips, or other overlays often do not meet the definition of "modal" and therefore keyboard and screen reader focus are not required to be maintained within when they are not truly "modal"\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Screen reader focus is lost or placed on the wrong element during user interaction, content refresh or update, or other reason\. \(focus\-lost\-screen\-reader, "issue", serious\)
- When the modal dialog is activated, screen reader focus is not placed on/in it\. \(focus\-modal\-none\-screen\-reader, "issue", serious\)
- When the modal dialog or similar element is closed, screen reader focus is not returned to the triggering element\. \(focus\-modal\-not\-returned\-screen\-reader, "issue", serious\)
- Screen reader focus does not follow visual focus or move to the intended target, such as with a "return to top" control\. \(focus\-not\-moved\-screen\-reader, "issue", moderate\)
- Screen reader focus falls on an interactive element that is hidden or empty\. \(focus\-on\-hidden\-item\-screen\-reader, "issue", serious\)
- The correct reading order of dynamically changed content is not programmatically determinable by screen readers\. \(new\-content\-bad\-location, "issue", serious\)
- Screen readers can read content outside the modal dialog\. \(reading\-order\-browse\-outside\-modal, "issue", serious\)
- Static text displayed and screen reader announcement do not match and the intended meaning of the content is changed\. \(reading\-order\-content\-mismatch, "issue", critical\)
- Informative \(static\) content is not readable by a screen reader\. \(reading\-order\-content\-skipped, "issue", critical\)
- The reading order does not make sense when CSS is disabled\. \(reading\-order\-css\-dependent, "issue", critical\)
- Content that is intended to be hidden from all users is readable with a screen reader\. \(reading\-order\-hidden\-content, "issue", serious\)
- The reading order of the layout table does not make sense when linearized\. \(reading\-order\-layout\-table\-non\-linear, "issue", critical\)
- The screen reader reading order of the content changes the intended meaning of the content\. \(reading\-order\-not\-logical, "issue", critical\)
- aria\-hidden="true" is used on informative content making it inaccessible to assistive technology\. \(semantic\-hidden, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

- Content in newly opened modal is auto announced \- Content in newly opened modal is automatically announced by screen reader\. \(new\-content\-modal\-not\-announced, "best\-practice", serious, May 2, 2018\)
- New content is automatically announced \- New content is automatically announced by screen reader\. \(new\-content\-not\-announced, "best\-practice", serious, May 2, 2018\)
- Add a link to footnote \- Consider adding a link from the footnote reference to the footnote content\. \(reading\-order\-footnote, "best\-practice", moderate, April 26, 2018\)
- Add return\-to\-trigger link to footnote \- Consider adding a return\-to\-trigger link from the footnote back to the original location\. \(reading\-order\-footnote\-return, "best\-practice", minor, January 11, 2017\)
- Reading order identical \- Reading order and visual order should match\. \(reading\-order\-visual\-order\-mismatch, "best\-practice", serious, March 26, 2013\)

1\.3\.1\.a

__1\.3\.1\.a Semantics__

__Overview__

- General \- Information, structure, and relationships that are presented visually must be conveyed programmatically or in text\. Semantic elements must be used according to their semantic meaning, not because of the way they appear visually\.
- HTML \- Information, structure, and relationships that are presented visually must be conveyed programmatically or in text\. Semantic elements must be used according to their semantic meaning, not because of the way they appear visually\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

The purpose of this checkpoint is to document issues related to Success Criterion 1\.3\.1 Information and Relationships that do not fall under any of the other 1\.3\.1 checkpoints for: tables, group labels, headings, and lists\.

Failures to log under this checkpoint include scenarios below\. All of these scenarios are relevant to a screen reader user’s experience and are usually found when reviewing content with a screen reader\.

- Information, structure, or relationship is presented visually but is not conveyed programmatically or in text\. This includes information that is conveyed through visual relationship, color \+ contrast, or other visual cue\. For example: 
	- Visual relationship: A chat interface shows a conversation between two or more people\. A sighted user can see who a comment is from by the visual relationship of the shape or color of the chat bubble and the chat participant it is "pointing" to, but a screen reader user doesn’t get that same information\.
	- Visual relationship: Comments on a blog post are visually presented such that one can tell which comments are replies to other comments because they are indented\. There is no use of list markup, heading markup, or text to convey that relationship to screen reader users
	- Color or Contrast: The difference between two elements is conveyed by color, but with enough contrast to pass 1\.4\.1 Use of Color\. However, the difference between the elements is not also conveyed to screen readers\.
- Text that is shown visually as being deleted or inserted is not identified for screen readers either programmatically or in text\.
- An incorrect ARIA landmark or HTML sectioning element is used\.
- Missing <blockquote> markup for block quotations\.
- Using semantic markup for visual purposes that misrepresents the meaning, structure, or relationships of the content\.
- Use of role="presentation" is on content that conveys semantic information, so the semantic information is not conveyed\.
- MAIN NAVIGATION area is missing semantics that convey its structure\. Possible ways to convey the group include using: 
	- list markup
	- headings
	- role="navigation"
	- <nav> element
	- menu with menuitem\(s\)
	- menubar with menuitem\(s\)

__Things to think about__

- SC 1\.3\.1 says Information, structure, and relationships conveyed through presentation can be programmatically determined or are available in text\. Do not forget the part "\.\.\.or in text" when evaluating this Success Criterion"\.
- Log issues related to tables, group labels, headings, and lists under the appropriate 1\.3\.1 checkpoint\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Use a screen reader to evaluate the issues described above\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Information, structure, or relationship is presented visually but is not conveyed programmatically or in text\. \(info\-relationship\-visual\-only, "issue", critical\)
- The element is a quotation, but is not marked as such\. \(quotation\-not\-indicated, "issue", moderate\)
- Text that is shown visually as being deleted or inserted is not identified for screen readers either programmatically or in text\. \(semantic\-del\-ins\-missing, "issue", serious\)
- Incorrect semantic markup is used\. \(semantic\-incorrect, "issue", serious\)
- Semantic markup has been used inappropriately and misrepresents the meaning, structure, or relationships of the content\. \(semantic\-markup\-used\-for\-presentation, "issue", serious\)
- A visual group of navigation links is not marked up semantically to convey their relationship\. Some possible solutions include using list\(s\), role of navigation, nav element, headings, menu or menubar\. \(semantic\-nav, "issue", moderate\)
- role="presentation" or "none" is used on content that conveys semantic information, so the semantic information is not conveyed\. \(semantic\-role\-presentation, "issue", critical\)
- USER AGENT: Use of DEL or INS element is not supported by all screen readers\. \(user\-agent\-semantic\-del\-ins, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Programmatically associate errors \- Error messages should be programmatically associated with the corresponding form field\. \(form\-error\-not\-associated, "best\-practice", serious, February 3, 2016\)
- Programmatically assoc instructions/descriptions \- When additional instructions/descriptions are provided for a control, they should be programmatically associated with the control\. \(instructions\-not\-associated, "best\-practice", serious, March 27, 2017\)
- Provide labels when multiple navigation regions exist \- When multiple navigation regions are present on a page, provide aria\-labels to distinguish the purpose of each\. \(nav\-sections\-not\-identified, "best\-practice", moderate, February 28, 2019\)
- Use text in addition to DEL or INS elements \- Use a text\-based method in addition to DEL or INS elements to convey the meaning of the deleted or inserted text\. The text can be provided for screen readers only or be visible on screen\. \(semantic\-del\-ins\-plus\-text, "best\-practice", moderate, February 7, 2022\)
- Use STRONG or EM instead of B or I \- Use STRONG to indicate importance\. Use EM to indicate emphasis\. Only use B or I as a last resort when no other element is more appropriate\. \(semantic\-strong\-or\-emphasis\-not\-used, "best\-practice", minor, April 24, 2017\)

1\.3\.1\.b

__1\.3\.1\.b Data Tables__

__Overview__

- General \- Data cells must be explicitly associated with header cells for data tables, and descriptive table captions and summaries may be provided where appropriate\.
- HTML \- Data cells must be explicitly associated with header cells for data tables, and descriptive table captions and summaries may be provided where appropriate\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Look for content that visually looks like it is arranged as a table\.
2. Determine whether the table is used as a presentation \(layout\) table or to display tabular \(data\) information\. 
	- For __presentation tables__, ensure that there are no th, caption, or summary elements, or scope or headers attributes\. 
		- __Note:__ Adding role="presentation" or role="none" to a layout table is a recommended best practice\. It is not a failure if it is not present\.
	- For data tables: 
		- Most two\-dimensional tables with at least three columns need both column and row headers\. Ask yourself if you were reading down a column with a screen reader, would you need to know the content of the first cell in that row to understand what the data in that cell relates to?
		- Ensure that table cells acting as headers \(USUALLY, but not always, bold, centered, on the top row and/or first column\) are identified as th elements\.
			- __Note:__ The scope attribute is not required for tables that have simple row and / or column headers\. It is not a failure if it is not present\.
			- Empty table header cells are not a failure\.
		- Complex tables, such as those with grouped headers \(merged row and/or column headers\) or complex headers \(data cells are merged or there are more than two levels of headers for any given dimension\), require additional table semantics\. See examples of various data table types and code techniques in [Deque University [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://dequeuniversity.com/class/semantic-structure/tables/)](https://dequeuniversity.com/class/semantic-structure/tables/)and the [WAI Tables Tutorial [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/tables/)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/tables/)\.

__Example Simple Data Table__

Simple, two\-dimensional table

__<th>__

__<th>__

__<th>__

__<th>__

<td>

<td>

__<th>__

<td>

<td>

__Things to think about__

A simple display of name value pairs is not required to be marked up as a data table\. For example, the following name / value pairs could simply be marked up using divs, spans, a list, or a layout table\.

Policy and Contact Information

