jenny@foo\.com

__Recommended tools to reveal table markup__

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Tables bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals HTML <table> elements\):

- Outline > Show Element Tag Names
- Outline > Outline Table Cells \(if tables are present, table cells will outline in red\)

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__ However, using a screen reader may be useful on complex tables\. 

- Using __NVDA__, hit the T key \(tables\) to cycle through tables on the page\. Use the UP and DOWN arrow keys to navigate through the cells of a given table\. You can also navigate between table cells in NVDA using the exact same key combinations for JAWS which are to hold down Ctrl \+ Alt and use the UP, DOWN, LEFT and RIGHT arrow keys to move from cell to cell\. 
- Using __JAWS__, hit the T key \(tables\) to cycle through tables on the page\. To navigate between cells, hold down Ctrl \+ Alt and use the UP, DOWN, LEFT and RIGHT arrow keys to move from cell to cell\. Use Ctrl \+ Alt \+ 5 \(on the numeric keypad\) to read the row and/or column headers for the current cell\. 
- Using __VoiceOver__, hit the VO and arrow keys to navigate through the tables in the page\. If a table has proper table headers, the column headers will be identified automatically, as the table is explored\. VoiceOver does not currently read row headers, though it will identify the row number\. Hitting the VO \+ C keys will read the column header\. __Note:__ VoiceOver also has difficulty with spanned row or column headers and when the top\-left cell is empty\. This often results in incorrect headers being read\. 

__Issue Descriptions__

- Data is arranged visually like a data table, but the screen reader does not read the required header cells along with the individual pieces of data content\. \(data\-table\-headers, "issue", critical\)
- The content visually appears to be a data table but is not marked up as one\. \(semantic\-data\-table, "issue", critical\)
- The data table has missing or incomplete header cell markup\. \(semantic\-data\-table\-headers, "issue", critical\)
- The headers \+ id association is not correct\. \(table\-complex\-association\-incorrect, "issue", serious\)
- A table is complex and does not use the headers \+ id method to mark up column and row header cells\. \(table\-complex\-no\-header\-associations, "issue", critical\)
- The first row of the table acts more like a caption for the table than an actual header row\. \(table\-data\-headers\-captiony, "issue", serious\)
- More than one table element is used to create a single table so header and data cell association is not correct and table navigation is broken\. \(table\-data\-multiple\-combined, "issue", critical\)
- A data cell is inappropriately marked as a table header cell\. \(table\-data\-not\-header, "issue", serious\)
- Layout table uses data table markup: TH or CAPTION elements or SUMMARY, HEADERS, or SCOPE attributes\. \(table\-layout\-header\-markup, "issue", serious\)
- The table uses the scope attribute incorrectly\. \(table\-simple\-scope\-incorrect, "issue", critical\)
- The scope attribute value is not a valid value\. Valid values are row, col, rowgroup, or colgroup\. \(table\-simple\-scope\-invalid, "issue", critical\)
- The scope attribute is used on TD elements\. This is obsolete\. Use a TH element instead\. \(table\-simple\-scope\-on\-td, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Table caption \- Tables should use the caption element to programmatically associate a visual name with the data table\. \(table\-caption\-not\-associated, "best\-practice", minor, February 12, 2015\)
- Complex table headers \- If a complex tables uses TH with HEADERS \+ ID association, avoid using the SCOPE attribute\. \(table\-complex\-multiple\-associations, "best\-practice", minor, February 12, 2015\)
- Avoid complex tables \- When a data table is complex, try breaking the complex table into separate simple tables\. \(table\-complex\-simplify, "best\-practice", moderate, March 19, 2015\)
- Avoid nested tables \- Layout tables should not be nested\. If nested data tables exist, reconsider the design to avoid nested tables\. \(table\-data\-nested, "best\-practice", moderate, March 19, 2015\)
- Avoid duplicate SUMMARY and CAPTION \- The table SUMMARY attribute and CAPTION element should not be identical\. \(table\-data\-summary\-caption, "best\-practice", minor, February 12, 2015\)
- Use role="presentation" or "none" on layout tables \- If a layout table exists, you should add role="presentation" or "none" on the TABLE element\. \(table\-layout\-improper\-role, "best\-practice", moderate, March 19, 2015\)
- Avoid layout tables \- Tables should only be used for tabular data\. Avoid using tables for layout\. \(table\-layout\-use\-css, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Consider data table for name value pairs \- Name value pairs may be marked up as a data table\. \(table\-name\-value\-pairs, "best\-practice", moderate, March 19, 2015\)
- Table header SCOPE \- A table that has both row and column headers should use the SCOPE attribute in each header\. \(table\-simple\-headers\-unscoped, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)

1\.3\.1\.c Programatic Labels \(Depreticated\)

1\.3\.1\.d

__1\.3\.1\.d Group Related Form Elements__

__Overview__

- General \- For groups of related controls where the individual labels for each control do not provide a sufficient description, additional group labels must be provided\.
- HTML \- For groups of related controls where the individual labels for each control do not provide a sufficient description, additional group labels must be provided\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate any form controls on the screen that visually have a group label, for example a group of radio buttons with a group label above\.
2. Determine if the purpose of the individual fields __cannot__ be determined without the group label\. 
	- Grouping controls is most important for related radio buttons and checkboxes\. 
		- Most commonly, the individual label associated with a radio or checkbox control does not fully convey the group's context, so the group label is required to be programmatically associated with the group of controls\.
		- Less commonly, the individual label for radio buttons or checkboxes does include clear instructions or distinct selections\. In this case, the group label \(if present\) is not required to be programmatically associated with the controls\.
	- It is much less common for other controls, such as text fields, to require a group label to understand the purpose of individual fields\. An example of when group labels may be required is if a form contains multiple address types \("shipping address" vs\. "billing address"\)\. In this case, a group label is required to understand which address group a particular "street address" or "city" field belongs to\.
3. For form groups where the purpose of the individual fields __cannot__ be determined without the group label, confirm that the fields are grouped and associated with the group label using one of the following techniques: 
	- Look at the code using the browser's DOM Inspect tool or use the [Forms bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/forms.html)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/forms.html)to identify <fieldset> and <legend> elements\.
	- Look at the code using the browser's DOM Inspect tool or use the [ARIA bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/aria.html)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/aria.html)to confirm that the elements are grouped within an element that has: 
		- The role="group" OR role="radiogroup" attribute AND
		- An aria\-labelledby attribute that points to the ID value on the group label OR an aria\-label whose value contains the group label\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual using a screen reader to test that group labels are programmatically associated with grouped fields when required\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The group label is not programmatically associated with its checkboxes\. The purpose of individual checkboxes in the group cannot be understood without the group label\. \(label\-group\-checkboxes\-not\-associated, "issue", serious\)
- The group of form controls is not associated with its group label\. The purpose of individual controls in the group cannot be understood without the group label\. \(label\-group\-not\-associated, "issue", serious\)
- The group label is not associated with its group of radio buttons\. The purpose of individual radio buttons in the group cannot be understood without the group label\. \(label\-group\-radio\-not\-associated, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Avoid FIELDSET with no LEGEND \- Each FIELDSET should have a LEGEND element\. \(legend\-missing, "best\-practice", minor, April 9, 2013\)

1\.3\.1\.e Headings

__1\.3\.1\.e Headings__

__Overview__

- General \- Headings used to describe sections of content must be semantically identified as such\.
- HTML \- Headings used to describe sections of content must be semantically identified as such\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Look for text that looks and functions as a heading\.
2. Ensure that every element on the screen that functions as a heading is marked as a heading\.
3. Ensure that the levels of the headings properly convey the structural hierarchy of the content\.
4. Ensure that content that does not function as a heading for other content is not marked as a heading\.

__Things to think about__

- Headings must function as headings for other content\. Just because text is big and bold doesn’t mean it is always an actual heading\.
- There does not always have to be content between headings\. It is permissible for a nested heading to immediately follow a heading\.
- While WCAG’s normative Success Criterion and informative Understanding Document language is clear that text that functions as a heading must be marked as a heading, it is surprisingly vague about how strict one should be about heading levels\. When evaluating heading levels, keep the following in mind: 
	- Skipping heading levels is not a failure\.
	- Multiple h1s is not necessarily a failure\.
	- Although rare, not every screen has to have an h1 \- or even any headings\.
	- To be a failure, heading levels must be out of order in such a way that the relative hierarchical structure of the page is misrepresented\. For example: 
		- An h2 follows an h4, where the h2 is clearly meant to be a subheading of the h4\.
		- A screen that has two headings that look and function at the same hierarchical level, but the second heading is marked as being a subheading of the first heading\.
		- A screen has two headings that look and function as if the second heading is a subheading to the first heading, but they are marked as being the same heading level\.

__Failure Example: out of order headings__

h1: My Computer Store  
h5: Products  
h2: Desktop computers  
h2: Laptop computers  
h2: Contact Us 

