Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals HTML headings\):

- Outline > Show Element Tag Names
- Outline > Outline Headings

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

1. Using __NVDA__ or __JAWS__, 
	1. Use the H key \(Headings\) to cycle through headings on the page, or hit the 1 to 6 keys to find heading of the corresponding levels \(if present\)\. 
		- Alternatively, use the NVDA \+ F7 key to prompt a dialog box showing all headings available in the page \(INS \+ F6 for JAWS\)\. 
2. Using __VoiceOver__, 
	1. Use the VO keys \+ Command \+ H to navigate through the headings in the page\. 
3. Make sure every element in the page that functions as a heading is spoken as such by the screen reader\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Heading levels are out of order in such a way that the structure of the content is not properly conveyed\. \(heading\-level\-order, "issue", serious\)
- Text appears and functions like a section heading but is not marked up as such\. \(semantic\-heading, "issue", serious\)
- Text that does not serve as a section heading is inappropriately coded as a heading\. \(semantic\-heading\-misused, "issue", serious\)
- Text appears and functions like a section heading but is not marked up as such\. \(semantic\-heading\-mobile, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Avoid empty headings \- All heading elements should have content\. \(heading\-empty, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)
- Avoid skipping heading levels \- Heading levels should not be skipped\. \(heading\-level\-increase, "best\-practice", moderate, April 23, 2013\)
- Avoid more than one H1 \- There should only be one H1 per page\. \(heading\-multiple\-h1s, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- At least one H1 \- There should be at least one H1 per page\. \(heading\-no\-h1, "best\-practice", moderate, April 23, 2013\)
- Headings \- Each page should have headings\. \(heading\-none, "best\-practice", serious, April 23, 2013\)

1\.3\.1\.f List

__1\.3\.1\.f Lists__

__Overview__

- General \- Lists must be semantically identified as such\.
- HTML \- Lists must be semantically identified as such\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate text that visually looks like a list\. A list is a number of connected items printed consecutively, typically one below the other\. A list often \(but not always\) has bullets, numbers or letters before each item\. 
	- __Note:__ According to the HTML specification, it is permissible for a list to contain zero or more list items, i\.e\. a list containing zero items or one list item is acceptable\.
2. Success Criteria 1\.3\.1 requires that structure that is conveyed through presentation be available programmatically OR in text\. This means that list structure MAY be conveyed by text \(e\.g\. actual text numbers or alternative text for an image\) without semantic structural markup such as <ul>/<li>\. See the Examples section below for some pass/fail scenarios\.
3. Check to see if a list is conveyed by text \(see the Examples section below\)\. For any lists that are NOT conveyed using text, check that the list is marked as a list using HTML or ARIA\. 
	- Use the [Lists bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/lists.html)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/lists.html)or browser Inspect/Inspect Element panel to ensure that text that serves as a list is marked as a list using one of the following: 
		- HTML elements: <ul>/<li>, <ol>/<li>, <dl>/<dt> OR
		- ARIA attributes role="list" and role="listitem"\. __Note:__ ARIA only has one type of list: unordered\.
4. Check that the list type is correct\. Types of lists include: 
	- Unordered list <ul> \- a list of items where the order of the items does not impact the meaning of the list content\.
	- Ordered list <ol> \- a list of items where the order of the items impacts the meaning of the list content\.
	- Description list <dl> \- a list used to pair a term with one or more descriptions such as terms and definitions, questions and answers, categories and topics, or any other groups of term\-description pairs\.
5. Check that list markup is not used on text that is not a functionally a list\. 
	- Use the [Lists bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/lists.html)](http://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets/lists.html)or browser Inspect/Inspect Element panel to ensure that only text that serves as a list is marked as a list\.

__Validation:__

1. Text that visually serves as a list is marked as a list using text, HTML, or ARIA\.
2. The list type \- unordered, ordered, description \- is appropriate\.
3. List markup is not used on text that is not a list\.

__Examples__

Example 1: No list markup required:  
I bought 3 things today: 1\. bread, 2\. butter, 3\. eggs\.

Example 2: No list markup required:  
I bought 3 things today: bread, butter, and eggs\.

Example 3: No list markup required because the numbers are in text and meet the bare minimum of WCAG 2\.0 SC 1\.3\.1:  
I bought 3 things today:   
1\. bread   
2\. butter   
3\. eggs

Example 4: List markup is required because visual presentation \(not text\) is being used to visually indicate a list:  
I bought 3 things today:   
bread   
butter   
eggs

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Using a screen reader ensure:

1. Text that visually serves as a list is read as a list\.
2. The list type \- unordered, ordered, description \- is appropriate\.
3. List markup is not used on text that is not a list\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The description list is not marked up properly\. \(semantic\-description\-list, "issue", serious\)
- Content appears like a list but is not marked up as such\. \(semantic\-list, "issue", serious\)
- The nested list is not marked properly\. \(semantic\-list\-nested, "issue", serious\)
- The list or list item is not marked up properly\. \(semantic\-list\-or\-item, "issue", serious\)
- List element has direct children that are not allowed inside UL or OL elements\. Lists must only directly contain LI, SCRIPT, or TEMPLATE elements\. \(semantic\-list\-unallowed\-children, "issue", moderate\)
- Content is not a list but it is marked as such\. \(semantic\-list\-used\-for\-presentation, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__1\.1\.1\.a Alternative Text \(Active Images\)__

__Overview__

- General \- All linked images must have a descriptive text alternative that serves the same purpose and presents the same information as the linked image\.
- HTML \- All linked images must have a descriptive text alternative that serves the same purpose and presents the same information as the linked image\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Note:__ If an <img>, <svg>, or an element with role="img" is missing a programmatic text alternative, the automated tests in axe Auditor \(axe\-core rules\) will create an issue under checkpoint 1\.1\.1\.b by default\. Testers using axe Auditor MAY choose to: Examine the automated issues and move them to a more relevant checkpoint depending on the type of image, and edit the automated issue to pick an issue from the issue description library and choose an appropriate remediation recommendation from the remediation recommendation library\.

1. Locate each image that can be activated as part of the user interface\. Common examples include: 
	1. Images inside a link
	2. Images inside a button
	3. <area> elements
	4. <input type=”image”>
2. Ensure that the image has an appropriate text alternative\.

__Things to think about__

- If the image is the only element providing an accessible name for a control, it must have a text alternative that describes the destination, purpose, or function of the control\.
- If the control also contains text content, ensure that one of the following is true: 
	- The image text alternative, together with the control's text content, conveys the purpose of the control\.
	- The image conveys the same information as the text content, so is redundant and therefore "decorative"\. The image should instead be assessed as part of the Alternative Text \(Decorative Images\) checkpoint\.
- The alt attribute is not the only way to provide an accessible name for an image or a control\. Valid alternatives also include: 
	- aria\-label="some text here" \(on the image or the control it is inside\)
	- aria\-labelledby="someID" \(on the image or the control it is inside\), where "someID" points to the ID given to other descriptive text in the page
	- CSS clipped text \(inside the control\)
	- title="some text here" \(on the image or the control it is inside\)
- <canvas> elements used to display images must have role="img" and a text alternative provided by aria\-label or aria\-labelledby pointing to text within the <canvas> element itself\.
- __Reality Check:__ If you replaced the active image with the text alternative, you would still understand the destination, purpose, or function of the control\.
- __Best Practice:__ If there is meaningful text embedded in the active image, the text alternative should contain that exact text\.

__Recommended tools to reveal an image’s text alternative include:__

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Images bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals alt and title attributes on <img> and <input type="text">\):

- Images > Display Alt Attributes
- Information > Display Title Attributes

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

__Using NVDA:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Ensure each image link has a text alternative value that properly describes the same purpose and presents the same information as the active image\. 

__Using VoiceOver:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the VO\+Command\+G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Verify that each image link has a text alternative value that properly describes the same purpose and presents the same information as the active image\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The image is changing dynamically but the text alternative doesn't correspond to the active image displayed\. \(alt\-text\-dynamic\-image\-inconsistent\-active, "issue", serious\)
- The text alternative does not contain the essential text in the active image, and therefore does not present the same information as the image\. \(alt\-text\-essential\-image\-text\-active, "issue", serious\)
- The text alternative does not serve the same purpose as the image\. \(alt\-text\-inconsistent, "issue", serious\)
- The text alternative for the active image is missing\. \(alt\-text\-missing\-active, "issue", critical\)
- A server\-side image map is missing an equivalent set of text links and/or text alternative to alert the user that these links are available\. \(image\-map\-equivalent, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

- Alt text exact \- When text is embedded in an image, the alt text should include the exact text that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-image\-text\-mismatch, "best\-practice", minor, July 16, 2013\)
- Avoid alt text that duplicates adjacent link text \- Alt text for a linked image should not duplicate the adjacent link text for the same resource / destination because it creates a stutter effect\. \(alt\-text\-redundancy\-adjacent\-link, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)
- Avoid alt text that duplicates link or button text \- Alt text for an image inside a link or button should not duplicate the text content of the link or button\. The alt text should be null\. \(alt\-text\-redundancy\-link\-text, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)
- Alt text length \- The alt text value should be less than 150 characters\. \(alt\-text\-too\-long, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Alt text for trademark \- The text alternative should include text to represent the trademark, service mark, registered trademark or copyright symbol that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-trademark, "best\-practice", minor, July 16, 2013\)
- Succinct alt text \- The alt text should succinctly describe the image content without being too verbose\. \(alt\-text\-verbose, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)

__1\.1\.1\.b Alternative Text \(Informative Images\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Images that convey content must have a descriptive text alternative that serves the same purpose and presents the same information as the image\.
- HTML \- Images that convey content must have a descriptive text alternative that serves the same purpose and presents the same information as the image\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Note:__ If an <img>, <svg>, or an element with role="img" is missing a programmatic text alternative, the automated tests in axe Auditor \(axe\-core rules\) will create an issue under checkpoint 1\.1\.1\.b\. by default\. Testers using axe Auditor MAY choose to: Examine the automated issues and move them to a more relevant checkpoint depending on the type of image, and edit the automated issue to pick an issue from the issue description library and choose an appropriate remediation recommendation from the remediation recommendation library\.

1. Locate each informative \(non\-active / non\-functional\) image\.
2. Ensure that the image has an appropriate text alternative that serves the same purpose and presents the same information as the image\.

__Things to think about__

- If the information presented by the image is provided elsewhere on the page in real text, it is permissible to treat the image as redundant or decorative\. See the checkpoint Alternative Text \(Decorative Images\)\.
- The alt attribute is not the only way to provide an accessible name for an image\. Valid alternatives also include: 
	- aria\-label="some text here"
	- aria\-labelledby="someID", where "someID" points to the ID given to other descriptive text in the page
	- CSS clipped text
	- title="some text here"
- <canvas> elements used to display images must have role="img" and a text alternative provided by aria\-label or aria\-labelledby pointing to text within the <canvas> element itself\.
- __Reality Check:__ If you replaced the informative image with the text alternative, you would still have access to all the meaningful information in that image\.
- __Best Practice:__ If there is meaningful text embedded in the image, the text alternative should contain that exact text\.

__Recommended tools to reveal an image's text alternative include:__

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Images bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals alt and title attributes on <img> and <input type="text">\):

- Images > Display Alt Attributes
- Information > Display Title Attributes

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

__Using NVDA:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Ensure each informative image has a text alternative value that properly describes the same purpose and presents the same information as the informative image\.

__Using VoiceOver:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the VO\+Command\+G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Verify that the informative image has a text alternative value that properly describes the same purpose and presents the same information as the informative image\.

__Examples__

- [A Simple Alt Text Decision Tree [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decision-tree/)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decision-tree/)

__Issue Descriptions__

- The text alternative does not present the same information as the image\. \(alt\-text\-different, "issue", serious\)
- The image is changing dynamically but the text alternative doesn't correspond to the informative image displayed\. \(alt\-text\-dynamic\-image\-inconsistent, "issue", serious\)
- The text alternative does not contain the essential text in the informative image, and therefore does not present the same information as the image\. \(alt\-text\-essential\-image\-text, "issue", serious\)
- The text alternative for the informative image is missing\. \(alt\-text\-missing, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

- Alt text exact \- When text is embedded in an image, the alt text should include the exact text that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-image\-text\-mismatch, "best\-practice", minor, July 16, 2013\)
- Alt text length \- The alt text value should be less than 150 characters\. \(alt\-text\-too\-long, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Alt text for trademark \- The text alternative should include text to represent the trademark, service mark, registered trademark or copyright symbol that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-trademark, "best\-practice", minor, July 16, 2013\)
- Succinct alt text \- The alt text should succinctly describe the image content without being too verbose\. \(alt\-text\-verbose, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)

__1\.1\.1\.c Alternative Text \(Complex Images\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Images that are too complex to be fully described in a short text alternative must have their purpose described using an extended text alternative\.
- HTML \- Images that are too complex to be fully described in a short text alternative must have their purpose described using an extended text alternative\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Note:__ If an <img>, <svg>, or an element with role="img" is missing a programmatic text alternative, the automated tests in axe Auditor \(axe\-core rules\) will create an issue under checkpoint 1\.1\.1\.b\. by default\. Testers using axe Auditor MAY choose to: Examine the automated issues and move them to a more relevant checkpoint depending on the type of image, and edit the automated issue to pick an issue from the issue description library and choose an appropriate remediation recommendation from the remediation recommendation library\.

1. Locate each complex image \(i\.e\. an image that cannot be accurately described in approximately 150 characters or less\)\.
2. Ensure that the image has a short text alternative that briefly describes the image\.
3. Ensure that a full \(long\) text description has also been provided\.

__Things to think about__

- Ideally, the short text alternative references where a full description can be found; however, it is not a failure if it does not\.
- The alt attribute is not the only way to provide an accessible name for an image\. Valid alternatives also include: 
	- aria\-label="some text here"
	- aria\-labelledby="someID", where "someID" points to the ID given to other descriptive text in the page
	- CSS clipped text
	- title="some text here"
- Options for providing a full \(long\) description include: 
	- Provide the long description in the context of the page itself\.
	- Provide a button that expands a collapsed region that contains the long description\.
	- Provide a button to open a dialog that contains the long description\.
	- Provide a link to a long description on another page via a normal link text\.
- If a complex image is interactive, understanding the information being provided by the interaction is essential to assessing whether a text alternative that may be present provides an adequate alternative\.
- A short text alternative is required using one of the recommended methods \(e\.g\., role="img" with aria\-label or aria\-labelledby and text within the <canvas> element\)\. It's a best practice to use aria\-describedby to point to the long description for <canvas> elements\.
- __Reality Check:__ If you replaced the complex image \(and any available interactions\) with the short text alternative and the long description you would still have access to all the meaningful information in the complex image\.

__Recommended tools to reveal an image's text alternative include:__

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Images bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals alt and title attributes on <img> and <input type="text">\):

- Images > Display Alt Attributes
- Information > Display Title Attributes

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

__Using a screen reader:__ 

1. Cycle through images on the page\. For NVDA, use the G key \(graphics\)\. For VoiceOver, VO\+Command\+G key \(graphics\)\.
2. If the short alt text description is not sufficient, ensure a long description has been provided\. Options for providing a full \(long\) description include: 
	- Provide the long description in the context of the page itself\.
	- Provide a button that expands a collapsed region that contains the long description\.
	- Provide a button to open a dialog that contains the long description\.
	- Provide a link to a long description on another page via a normal link text\.

__Examples__

- [A Simple Alt Text Decision Tree [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decision-tree/)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decision-tree/)

__Issue Descriptions__

- The complex image is missing both short and detailed text alternatives\. \(alt\-text\-complex\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- The detailed textual description of the complex image is not adequate to convey its full meaning\. \(alt\-text\-longdesc\-inadequate, "issue", serious\)
- The detailed textual description of the complex image is missing\. \(alt\-text\-longdesc\-missing, "issue", critical\)
- A short text alternative for a complex image is missing\. \(alt\-text\-short\-text\-missing, "issue", serious\)
- The short text alternative for the complex image is not appropriate or meaningful\. \(alt\-text\-short\-text\-not\-meaningful, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Alt text length \- The alt text value should be less than 150 characters\. \(alt\-text\-too\-long, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Alt text for trademark \- The text alternative should include text to represent the trademark, service mark, registered trademark or copyright symbol that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-trademark, "best\-practice", minor, December 31, 2014\)
- Succinct alt text \- The alt text should succinctly describe the image content without being too verbose\. \(alt\-text\-verbose, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)

__1\.1\.1\.d Alternative Text \(Decorative Images\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Images that do not convey content, are decorative, or with content that is already conveyed in text must not be given a text alternative that is exposed to assistive technology\.
- HTML \- Images that do not convey content, are decorative, or with content that is already conveyed in text must be coded to be ignored by assistive technology\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__NOTE:__ If an <img>, <svg>, or an element with role="img" is missing a programmatic text alternative, the automated tests in axe Auditor \(axe\-core rules\) will create an issue under checkpoint 1\.1\.1\.b\. by default\. Testers using axe Auditor MAY choose to: Examine the automated issues and move them to a more relevant checkpoint depending on the type of image, and edit the automated issue to pick an issue from the issue description library and choose an appropriate remediation recommendation from the remediation recommendation library\.

1. Locate each decorative image\.
2. Ensure each redundant, unimportant, or decorative image has been implemented in such a way that it can be ignored by assistive technologies \(i\.e\. such as screen readers\)\.

__Things to think about__

- There are multiple ways to code images so they are ignored by assistive technologies: 
	- The image is implemented as a CSS background image\.
	- An <img> is given an empty alt attribute value \(alt=""\) and no title attribute\.
	- An <img> is given role="none", role="presentation" or aria\-hidden="true"\.
	- An <svg> or icon font is given aria\-hidden="true"\.

__Recommended tools to reveal an image’s text alternative include:__

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Images bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals alt and title attributes on <img> and <input type="text">\):

- Images > Display Alt Attributes
- Information > Display Title Attributes

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

__Using NVDA:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Ensure each decorative non\-text content is ignored because it is assigned an empty alt attribute value \(alt=””\) or is implemented in CSS\. 

__Using VoiceOver:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the VO\+Command\+G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Ensure each decorative non\-text content is ignored because it is assigned an empty alt attribute value \(alt=””\) or is implemented in CSS\.

__Examples__

- [A Simple Alt Text Decision Tree [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decision-tree/)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/decision-tree/)

__Issue Descriptions__

- The decorative image is not hidden from screen readers\. \(alt\-text\-decorative, "issue", moderate\)
- The decorative image is not hidden from screen readers\. \(alt\-text\-decorative\-inappropriate, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Avoid alt text that duplicates adjacent text \- Alt text for an image should not duplicate the adjacent text\. \(alt\-text\-redundancy, "best\-practice", minor, December 11, 2023\)
- Avoid alt text that duplicates adjacent link text \- Alt text for a linked image should not duplicate the adjacent link text for the same resource / destination because it creates a stutter effect\. \(alt\-text\-redundancy\-adjacent\-link, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)
- Avoid alt text that duplicates link or button text \- Alt text for an image inside a link or button should not duplicate the text content of the link or button\. The alt text should be null\. \(alt\-text\-redundancy\-link\-text, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)

1\.1\.1\.e Alternative Text\(CSS Images\)\(Deprecated\)

1\.1\.1\.f Alternative Text\(Input Images\)\(Deperecated\)

  
__1\.1\.1\.g Alternative Text \(Captcha\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Interactions designed to verify human users must not rely on a single sense to be successfully completed and alternate forms using different modalities must be provided to address the needs of people with different disabilities\.
- HTML \- Captcha tests must have descriptive text alternatives describing the purpose of the CAPTCHA, and alternate forms using different modalities must be provided to address the needs of people with different disabilities\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist
- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate the CAPTCHA image\.
2. Ensure there is an appropriate short text alternative describing the purpose of the CAPTCHA\.
3. Ensure there is an option to complete the CAPTCHA without vision \(the alternative is usually an audio CAPTCHA, but modalities can differ\)\.

__Things to think about__

The text alternative referred to in this checkpoint is NOT a text alternative for each CAPTCHA image\. Providing a text alternative on each image would make the CAPTCHA operable by robots, thus defeating its purpose\. In this case the text alternative would describe the overall purpose of the CAPTCHA, and alternate forms using different modalities would be provided to address the needs of people with different disabilities\.

__Recommended tools to reveal an image’s text alternative include:__

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Images bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

[Web Developer browser extension [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)](http://chrispederick.com/work/web-developer/)\(Note limited capabilities: only reveals alt and title attributes on <img> and <input type="text">\):

- Images > Display Alt Attributes
- Information > Display Title Attributes

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

__Using NVDA:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Ensure each CAPTCHA file is assigned an appropriate short text alternative describing the purpose of the CAPTCHA\.
3. Ensure there is an option to complete the CAPTCHA without vision \(the alternative is usually an audio CAPTCHA, but modalities can differ\)\.

__Using VoiceOver:__ 

1. To cycle through images on the page, hit the VO\+Command\+G key \(graphics\)\.
2. Ensure each CAPTCHA file is assigned an appropriate short text alternative describing the purpose of the CAPTCHA\.
3. Ensure there is an option to complete the CAPTCHA without vision \(the alternative is usually an audio CAPTCHA, but modalities can differ\)\.

__Reality Check:__ You can close your eyes and using the screen reader and keyboard alone, you can complete the CAPTCHA using the non\-visual option \(usually that will be an audio option, but other options are acceptable too\)\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The text alternative for the CAPTCHA image does not describe its purpose\. \(alt\-text\-inappropriate\-captcha, "issue", serious\)
- The text alternative for the CAPTCHA image is missing\. \(alt\-text\-missing\-captcha, "issue", serious\)
- There is only one option for the CAPTCHA task and it requires vision to complete it successfully\. \(captcha\-requires\-vision, "issue", critical\)

__Best Practices__

- Alt text length \- The alt text value should be less than 150 characters\. \(alt\-text\-too\-long, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Alt text for trademark \- The text alternative should include text to represent the trademark, service mark, registered trademark or copyright symbol that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-trademark, "best\-practice", minor, December 31, 2014\)
- Succinct alt text \- The alt text should succinctly describe the image content without being too verbose\. \(alt\-text\-verbose, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)

__1\.1\.1\.h Alternative Text \(Audio or Video\)__

__Overview__

- General \- Text alternatives must be provided to identify the purpose of the video or audio content\.
- HTML \- Text alternatives must be provided to identify the purpose of the video or audio content\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist
- Hearing or hearing assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__Looking at the page:__ 

1. Locate the multimedia object \(audio or video\)\.
2. Alternative/Identifying Text \- there is a short description \(like a title, heading, sentence or link text\) that identifies what the topic of the audio or video is\. 

__Reality Check:__ I know \(at a high level\) what the topic is for this multimedia content before I play it\. 

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

__Using NVDA:__ 

1. To cycle through multimedia files on the page, hit the O key \(object\)\.
2. Ensure each audio or video file is assigned a text alternative that describes the video or audio and/or gives its title\.

__Using VoiceOver:__ 

1. To cycle through multimedia files on the page, hit the VO\+Command\+O key \(objects\)\.
2. Verify that each audio or video file is assigned a text alternative that describes the video or audio and/or gives its title\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The text alternative is not appropriate\. \(alt\-text\-inappropriate, "issue", serious\)
- There is no text alternative to describe the media file\. \(alt\-text\-missing\-media\-file, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Alt text exact \- When text is embedded in an image, the alt text should include the exact text that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-image\-text\-mismatch, "best\-practice", minor, July 16, 2013\)
- Alt text length \- The alt text value should be less than 150 characters\. \(alt\-text\-too\-long, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2013\)
- Alt text for trademark \- The text alternative should include text to represent the trademark, service mark, registered trademark or copyright symbol that is embedded in the image\. \(alt\-text\-trademark, "best\-practice", minor, December 31, 2014\)
- Succinct alt text \- The alt text should succinctly describe the image content without being too verbose\. \(alt\-text\-verbose, "best\-practice", minor, March 19, 2015\)

__2\.4\.4\.a Link Purpose \(In Context\)__

__Overview__

- General \- The purpose of each link must be understandable from the link text alone, or from the link text and its programmatic context, unless the link purpose is ambiguous to users in general\.
- HTML \- The purpose of each link must be understandable from the link text alone, or from the link text and its programmatic context, unless the link purpose is ambiguous to users in general\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify all links on the screen\.
2. Identify any links that DO NOT meet AT LEAST ONE of the following criteria: 
	- The visible link text \(or alternative text for an image link or image map link\) alone is sufficient to describe the purpose of the link\.
	- The visible link text \(or alternative text for an image link or image map link\) plus the text in the same sentence, paragraph, list item, table cell, or table header for the cell is sufficient to describe the purpose of the link\.
3. For each link that DOES NOT meet one of the above criteria \(including image links and image maps\), check the accessible name for the link to see if the visible link text has been supplemented by an ARIA or title attribute or screen reader only text\.
4. __Exception:__ There is an exception to this checkpoint for where the purpose of the link would be ambiguous to users in general\. This would include situations where the purpose of the link is supposed to be unknown or obscured, for instance in a game\.

__Things to think about__

- Apply this Checkpoint 2\.4\.4\.a Link Purpose \(In Context\) to controls that function as navigation links \(i\.e\. navigate within the same page or screen or to a different page or screen\) even if they have a different role such as "button" or are missing a role\.
- Apply Checkpoint 2\.4\.6\.b Descriptive Labels to non\-link controls even if they have a link role or are missing a role\.
- There are multiple ways to provide supplemental information to link text including: 
	- aria\-label="some text here" \(on the <a> element or an image inside it\)\. Note: aria\-label will completely replace any other link text inside the link\.
	- aria\-labelledby="someID" \(on the <a> element\), where "someID" points to the ID given to other descriptive text in the page\. Note: aria\-labelledby will completely replace any other link text inside the link\.
	- aria\-describedby="someID" \(on the <a> element\), where "someID" points to the ID given to other descriptive text in the page\. Note: aria\-describedby will supplement \(not replace\) the link text inside the link\.
	- CSS clipped text \(inside the <a> element\)\.
	- LEAST PREFERRED OPTION: title="some text here" \(on the <a> element\)\. Note: a title attribute will supplement \(not replace\) the link text inside the link, but will ONLY be read by most screen readers when tabbing to the link, not when reading by arrow keys\. Therefore it is strongly discouraged as a method to supplement visible link text\.

__Recommended tools to reveal the accessible name of a link include:__

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

Use a screen reader to read the link name\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

1. Using a screen reader like NVDA or JAWS, pull up a list of links \(NVDA key \+ F7 or F7 for JAWS\)\. 
	1. Check if the link text for each link adequately describes its purpose, without other contextual information\.
2. For a link whose text, by itself, does not convey the purpose: 
	1. Check if tabbing to it announces contextual information \.\.\. this should happen if the title attribute or ARIA attributes are used\.
	2. Otherwise, use screen\-reader keys to check if the immediate context of the link \(sentence, paragraph, list item, associated row/column header cells\) provides supplemental text properly describing the purpose of the link\.
3. __Exception:__ There is an exception to this checkpoint for where the purpose of the link would be ambiguous to users in general\. This would include situations where the purpose of the link is supposed to be unknown or obscured, for instance in a game\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The text alternative for each area of the image map is not unique\. \(alt\-text\-image\-map\-nonunique, "issue", serious\)
- The purpose of the link is not clear within its programmatically determined link context\. \(link\-purpose\-unclear, "issue", serious\)
- Multiple links with identical link text but different destinations are present, and the purpose of each link is not clear within its programmatically determined link context\. \(link\-purpose\-unclear\-multiple, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Meaningful link text \- Link text should be clear from the link text alone\. While WCAG 2\.0 Success Criteria 2\.4\.4 allows the meaning to be taken within the context of the enclosing sentence, paragraph, list item or data table, it is better for all users if the link text is clear from the link text alone\. \(link\-purpose\-unidentifiable\-from\-text, "best\-practice", moderate, March 26, 2013\)

__2\.4\.6\.a Descriptive Headings__

__Overview__

- General \- Headings must be informative\.
- HTML \- Headings must be informative\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Locate all text on the screen that functions as a heading\.
2. Ensure each heading: 
	- Provides a clear description of the content in that section\.
	- Gives users an effective overview of the content and its organization\.

__Things to think about__

- This checkpoint does not require a screen to have headings, but if headings are provided, they must be descriptive\.
- The proper markup of headings is covered by checkpoint 1\.3\.1\.e
- An empty heading element is not a failure of the checkpoint\.

__Failure Examples__

- On Bob's Used Cars home page, the H1 is "Come on down\!" which doesn't convey that the page is for selling used cars\.
- On a movie review page, there are multiple headings of "Movie", each with different movie reviews below\.
- On a recruiter's webpage, several jobs are listed but each has the same H3 heading of "Open Position"\.
- A restaurant webpage has sufficient H3 heading text for dinner items; however the accessible name for all is overwritten by aria\-label="meal 1", "meal 2", etc\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

Follow the testing methodology for Desktop Web \- Manual\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- The heading does not describe the topic or purpose of the content that follows it\. \(heading\-not\-descriptive, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__2\.4\.6\.b Descriptive Labels__

__Overview__

- General \- Labels used to identify specific components within content must be descriptive\.
- HTML \- Labels used to identify specific components within content must be descriptive\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Visually identify each form control or interactive component on the screen\. 
	1. Verify that each control or component has a visible text OR graphic label that describes its purpose\.
	2. Verify that the control or component's accessible name also describes its purpose\.
	3. If there is no visible label, log the issue under 3\.3\.2\.a\.
2. Look for controls or interactive components that are used over and over on the screen\. These might need to provide additional information beyond what is provided visually\. For example, multiple "delete" or "purchase" buttons will need additional information about what is being deleted or purchased\. 
	1. Confirm that the label \(accessible name\) has additional context to sufficiently describe the purpose of the control\. Options include: 
		- The accessible name itself
		- An accessible description \(e\.g\. via aria\-describedby or a title attribute\)
		- A programmatically associated table header
		- A legend if it is in a fieldset
		- The accessible name of a semantic grouping if it is in an HTML sectioning element or ARIA landmark
		- The accessible name of a <video> element if it is a control in a video element
3. For each iframe and/or frame found in the page, verify that the value provided in the title or aria\-label attribute describes its purpose\.

__Things to think about__

- Apply this Checkpoint 2\.4\.6\.b Descriptive Labels to non\-link controls even if they have a link role or are missing a role\.
- Apply Checkpoint 2\.4\.4\.a Link Purpose \(In Context\) to controls that function as navigation links \(i\.e\. navigate within the same page or screen or to a different page or screen\) even if they have a different role such as "button" or are missing a role\.
- An icon MAY serve as a label\. Examples of common icons that label form fields \(or user controls\) include: magnifying glass \(for search\), 3 horizontal lines on top of each other \(hamburger menu\), gear \(preferences or settings\), trash can \(delete or view trash depending on context\)\. Remember, these are just a few examples\.

__Recommended methods to reveal the accessible name and description of a control include:__

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

Use a screen reader to read the component's name and description\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Referencing the Desktop testing methodology above, with a screen reader turned on, confirm that each control or component's label has enough context to sufficiently describe its purpose\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Multiple controls have the same labels/names but perform different actions\. This is not conveyed via text associated with individual controls\. \(buttons\-confusing, "issue", serious\)
- The label does not convey the purpose of the control\. \(label\-not\-descriptive, "issue", serious\)
- The programmatic label does not convey the purpose of the control\. The programmatic label and visual label do not convey consistent information\. \(label\-programmatic\-not\-descriptive, "issue", serious\)
- The visible label does not convey the purpose of the control\. The programmatic label and visual label do not convey consistent information\. \(label\-visible\-not\-descriptive, "issue", serious\)
- The iframe's title is not unique \- another iframe on the same page has the same title\. \(title\-iframe\-duplicate, "issue", moderate\)
- The title for the iframe is not meaningful\. \(title\-iframe\-not\-meaningful, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Button's programmatic name equals visible text label \- Button's programmatic name and visible text label should be identical\. \(button\-value\-text\-mismatch, "best\-practice", minor, May 23, 2018\)

__2\.4\.6\.b Descriptive Labels__

__Overview__

- General \- Labels used to identify specific components within content must be descriptive\.
- HTML \- Labels used to identify specific components within content must be descriptive\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Testing for this checkpoint can be accomplished using either the "Manual" methodology or the "Assistive Technology" methodology or a combination of both\.

1. Visually identify each form control or interactive component on the screen\. 
	1. Verify that each control or component has a visible text OR graphic label that describes its purpose\.
	2. Verify that the control or component's accessible name also describes its purpose\.
	3. If there is no visible label, log the issue under 3\.3\.2\.a\.
2. Look for controls or interactive components that are used over and over on the screen\. These might need to provide additional information beyond what is provided visually\. For example, multiple "delete" or "purchase" buttons will need additional information about what is being deleted or purchased\. 
	1. Confirm that the label \(accessible name\) has additional context to sufficiently describe the purpose of the control\. Options include: 
		- The accessible name itself
		- An accessible description \(e\.g\. via aria\-describedby or a title attribute\)
		- A programmatically associated table header
		- A legend if it is in a fieldset
		- The accessible name of a semantic grouping if it is in an HTML sectioning element or ARIA landmark
		- The accessible name of a <video> element if it is a control in a video element
3. For each iframe and/or frame found in the page, verify that the value provided in the title or aria\-label attribute describes its purpose\.

__Things to think about__

- Apply this Checkpoint 2\.4\.6\.b Descriptive Labels to non\-link controls even if they have a link role or are missing a role\.
- Apply Checkpoint 2\.4\.4\.a Link Purpose \(In Context\) to controls that function as navigation links \(i\.e\. navigate within the same page or screen or to a different page or screen\) even if they have a different role such as "button" or are missing a role\.
- An icon MAY serve as a label\. Examples of common icons that label form fields \(or user controls\) include: magnifying glass \(for search\), 3 horizontal lines on top of each other \(hamburger menu\), gear \(preferences or settings\), trash can \(delete or view trash depending on context\)\. Remember, these are just a few examples\.

__Recommended methods to reveal the accessible name and description of a control include:__

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

Use a screen reader to read the component's name and description\.

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Referencing the Desktop testing methodology above, with a screen reader turned on, confirm that each control or component's label has enough context to sufficiently describe its purpose\.

__Examples__

No examples

__Issue Descriptions__

- Multiple controls have the same labels/names but perform different actions\. This is not conveyed via text associated with individual controls\. \(buttons\-confusing, "issue", serious\)
- The label does not convey the purpose of the control\. \(label\-not\-descriptive, "issue", serious\)
- The programmatic label does not convey the purpose of the control\. The programmatic label and visual label do not convey consistent information\. \(label\-programmatic\-not\-descriptive, "issue", serious\)
- The visible label does not convey the purpose of the control\. The programmatic label and visual label do not convey consistent information\. \(label\-visible\-not\-descriptive, "issue", serious\)
- The iframe's title is not unique \- another iframe on the same page has the same title\. \(title\-iframe\-duplicate, "issue", moderate\)
- The title for the iframe is not meaningful\. \(title\-iframe\-not\-meaningful, "issue", moderate\)

__Best Practices__

- Button's programmatic name equals visible text label \- Button's programmatic name and visible text label should be identical\. \(button\-value\-text\-mismatch, "best\-practice", minor, May 23, 2018\)

__2\.5\.3\.a Label in Name__

__Overview__

- General \- For each user interface component with a label that includes text or an image of text, the accessible name must match or include the visible text in the label\.
- HTML \- For each user interface component with a label that includes text or an image of text, the accessible name must match or include the visible text in the label\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

Notes:

- This checkpoint ONLY applies to controls that have a __visible text or image of text label__\.
- For the purposes of this checkpoint, when a text field has placeholder text as its only programmatic label, consider the placeholder value to be the field's visible label\.
- If a control with a visible text label fails checkpoint 4\.1\.2\.a because the text label is not programmatically associated with the control, it also fails this checkpoint, because there is no accessible name for the label to be in\.

1. For every control that has a visible text or image of text label, compare the accessible name of the element to the visible text\.
2. Ensure the entire onscreen visible text label is EITHER: 
	- An exact match to the accessible name OR
	- Is contained within the accessible name \(in the same order presented visually with no words inserted in between\)
3. __Best Practice:__ It is a best practice \(but not a requirement\) to have the text of the visible label at the start of the accessible name\.

__Recommended methods to reveal the accessible name of a control include:__

Browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools > [Accessibility Pane [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/accessibility/reference/#pane)
- Firefox Web Developer Tools > [Accessibility Inspector [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Tools/Accessibility_inspector)

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

Screen reader testing is not applicable for this checkpoint\. Speech input testing is optional for this checkpoint\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- Accessible name contains the visible label text, but one or more other words is interspersed in the label\. \(speech\-accessible\-name\-interspersed, "issue", serious\)
- Accessible name does not contain the visible label text\. \(speech\-accessible\-name\-mismatch, "issue", serious\)
- The visible label is not in the accessible name because the label is not associated with the control\. \(speech\-accessible\-name\-missing, "issue", serious\)
- Accessible name contains the visible label text, but the words of the visible label are not in the same order as they are in the accessible name\. \(speech\-accessible\-name\-order, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Accessible name begins with visible label text \- The accessible name should begin with the same text that is in the visible label\. \(speech\-accessible\-name\-other\-words\-before, "best\-practice", moderate, July 3, 2018\)

__4\.1\.3\.a Status Messages__

__Overview__

- General \- Status messages must be programmatically determinable through role or properties such that they can be presented to the user by assistive technologies without receiving focus\.
- HTML \- Status messages must be programmatically determinable through role or properties such that they can be presented to the user by assistive technologies without receiving focus\.

__Testing Methodology__

Required for testing:

- Sight or sighted assist

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

1. Identify any status messages that can appear on this page/screen\. 
	- Status messages are defined as a change in content: 
		- That provides information to the user on the success or results of an action \(e\.g\. "Request submitted"\), on the waiting state of an application \(e\.g\. a content loading indicator\), on the progress of a process \(e\.g\. a file download indicator\), or on the existence of errors \(e\.g\. error message or failure to load page content or a process failure\)\.
		- Does not involve a change in focus such as moving focus to the message, to another window or tab, or opening a new page\.
		- See [status message examples [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/status-messages.html#examples)](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/status-messages.html#examples)\.
2. Trigger each status message and confirm that the newly added status message is announced by NVDA\. 

__Issue Descriptions__

- Status message is not automatically announced by the screen reader\. \(status\-msg\-not\-auto\-announced, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

No best practices

__2\.4\.2\.a Titles on Pages__

__Overview__

- General \- Screens must have descriptive and informative titles\.
- HTML \- Screens must have descriptive and informative titles\.

__Testing Methodology__

__Desktop Web \- Manual__

__NOTE:__ If a page is missing a <title> element in the <head>, the automated tests in axe Auditor \(axe\-core rules\) will create an issue\.

1. Ensure the value provided in the <title> element properly describes the content or purpose of the page\.
2. __Best Practice:__ It is a best practice that the page title is unique\.

__Recommended tools to reveal a page title__

Look at / hover over the browser "tab"\.

Code inspection in browser inspection tools:

- Chrome Developer Tools
- Firefox Web Developer Tools

Bookmarklets:

- [ANDI bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)](https://www.ssa.gov/accessibility/andi/help/install.html)
- [Paul Adam Page Title bookmarklet [![opens in a new window](data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAA0AAAANBAMAAACAxflPAAAAD1BMVEX///8BLMwBLMwBLMwBLMzPU4wZAAAABHRSTlMATL/leS62vgAAAEFJREFUCJljYGBQcXFxUWBgYHBkYGB0ANIOzMYGYJqBGUiruDgwGABpR6A4A5AGQgOQnANUvQNUP0QHAsNMg5oOAE00Cvw94JmgAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)](https://pauljadam.com/bookmarklets.html)

__Desktop Web \- Assistive Technology__

__Screen reader testing is optional for this checkpoint\.__

1. With NVDA turned on, load or reload the webpage\.
2. Ensure the page title spoken is an accurate description of the screen's content or purpose\.
3. __Best Practice:__ It is a best practice that the page title is unique\.

__Issue Descriptions__

- The TITLE element on this page is empty\. \(title\-empty, "issue", serious\)
- This page is missing a TITLE element\. \(title\-missing, "issue", serious\)
- There is no screen title or main heading at the beginning of the screen to identify the purpose of the screen\. \(title\-missing\-native\-mobile, "issue", serious\)
- The TITLE element on this page does not identify the contents or purpose of the page\. \(title\-not\-meaningful, "issue", serious\)
- The screen title or main heading does not identify the purpose of the screen\. \(title\-not\-meaningful\-native\-mobile, "issue", serious\)

__Best Practices__

- Unique page titles \- Each page title on a site should be unique within the website or cohesive context of a website\. \(title\-not\-unique, "best\-practice", moderate, September 3, 2015\)

